## 6. Responsive Design Requirements

The interface must work beautifully on desktop, tablet, and mobile.

Do not treat responsiveness as an afterthought.

### Desktop
- Use a balanced content width and intentional page margins.
- Take advantage of wider layouts for maps, timelines, issue queues, and data visualizations.
- Maintain clear alignment across sections.
- Avoid unnecessarily oversized components.

### Tablet
- Reflow multi-column sections intelligently.
- Keep important filters and actions accessible.
- Reduce visual clutter without removing essential functionality.

### Mobile
- Use a compact navigation pattern with an accessible menu.
- Make the primary reporting action easy to reach.
- Convert complex tables into readable rows or stacked information layouts.
- Make forms comfortable to complete on touchscreens.
- Prevent horizontal overflow.
- Keep buttons and controls large enough to interact with comfortably.
- Ensure filters work well in a drawer or expandable panel.
- Simplify maps and charts when necessary.
- Preserve all important functionality rather than merely shrinking the desktop layout.

Test the actual pages at representative viewport widths, including approximately 375px, 768px, and 1440px.

