## 9. Interactions and Micro-Animations

Use animation to clarify actions, not to decorate every element.

Appropriate examples:
- Subtle navigation transitions.
- Smooth filter expansion.
- Clear loading indicators.
- A restrained entrance animation for selected content.
- Small feedback transitions when an issue is submitted or a status changes.
- Gentle chart transitions when data changes.

Avoid:
- Scroll hijacking.
- Excessive parallax.
- Constantly floating elements.
- Animated backgrounds.
- Long entrance sequences.
- Animations that delay access to information.

Respect the user's reduced-motion preference.

Every interactive element must provide clear feedback and have a meaningful function.

---

## 10. Accessibility and Usability

Meet practical accessibility standards throughout the application.

- Use semantic landmarks and heading levels.
- Associate labels with form controls.
- Support keyboard navigation.
- Make focus states clearly visible.
- Maintain sufficient color contrast.
- Provide accessible names for icon-only controls.
- Do not use color as the only indicator of severity or status.
- Make validation errors understandable to screen readers.
- Ensure dialogs, menus, and drawers can be operated accessibly.
- Use descriptive text for important images and meaningful charts.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.

The design must be beautiful without sacrificing usability.

---

## 11. Content and Visual Authenticity

Write clear, specific interface copy.

Avoid generic phrases such as:
- "Empowering communities through innovation."
- "Revolutionizing the future with AI."
- "Your all-in-one civic solution."
- "Unlock the power of intelligent insights."

Use concrete language that explains what the user can do.

For example:
- "Report a problem on your street."
- "See which issues need attention."
- "12 reports are linked to this issue." Only display this if the actual data supports it.
- "This issue was updated yesterday." Only display this if the timestamp supports it.

Use realistic visual hierarchy and actual application data.

If no data exists, create useful empty states rather than populating the application with invented civic incidents.

